6875, 0.037506103515625, -0.033935546875, -0.0279388427734375, -0.0015506744384765625, 0.0083160400390625, -0.00452423095703125, -0.0123138427734375, 0.02197265625, -0.007015228271484375, 0.003040313720703125, -0.01522064208984375, 0.00884246826171875, 0.015533447265625, 0.028228759765625, 0.0025997161865234375, -0.0201263427734375, 0.0003628730773925781, 0.00951385498046875, 0.030914306640625, -0.004711151123046875, 0.0191497802734375, -0.003917694091796875, -0.01047515869140625, 0.01058197021484375, 0.001895904541015625, 0.0172119140625, -0.007587432861328125, 0.0239105224609375, 0.035980224609375, -0.02655029296875, -0.012908935546875, 0.0030345916748046875, 0.007541656494140625, 0.006267547607421875, 0.0164031982421875, 0.0133209228515625, -0.0192413330078125, -0.0021152496337890625, -0.019744873046875, -0.007701873779296875, 0.00200653076171875, -0.036895751953125, 0.01508331298828125, -0.06402587890625, 0.004596710205078125, -0.004695892333984375, -0.0164337158203125, 0.004352569580078125, -0.0328369140625, -0.005527496337890625, -0.01377105712890625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents are systems that systematically enhance their problem-solving abilities by learning incrementally from successive streams of tasks. These agents persistently store and update natural language memories\u2014summaries and feedback after each episode\u2014which they can later consult to improve future performance. Such methods have demonstrated strong results across a variety of domains, including robotics, virtual assistants, and reasoning tasks, where adaptability and cumulative learning are crucial (see Bengio et al., 2009; Wang et al., 2023; Lee et al., 2022). However, while much attention has focused on mean performance, an important open question is the *reliability* of these agents: specifically, the *consistency* (similarity of performance across repeated runs with unchanged settings) and *robustness* (stability when facing different randomizations or task orderings) of their improvements. These reliability issues are closely related to agent *fragility*\u2014the tendency for small variations, such as early mistakes or task sequence changes, to lead to substantial fluctuations or breakdowns in performance.\n\n---\n**Related Work and Operational Definitions**\n\nPrior work on continual and curriculum learning (Bengio et al., 2009) has emphasized the benefits of orderings where easier tasks precede harder ones, but often neglects analysis of statistical stability. In this study, we focus explicitly on how memory-based agents handle diverse task sequences and how their memory mechanisms may introduce new *failure modes*\u2014patterns or classes of errors that persist or repeat due to breakdowns in memory usage or feedback loops (cf. Wang et al., 2023).\n\nWe use the following operational concepts (each illustrated by an example) to guide our analyses:\n- *Consistency*: Minimal variation in measured outcomes (e.g., completion rates, average rewards) across repeated runs with identical settings. For example, a consistent agent would solve ~70% of tasks in repeated runs, with little spread.\n- *Robustness*: The maintenance of agent performance in the face of external variation, such as task sequence randomization or stochastic initializations. For instance, robust agents maintain similar completion rates even if the order of tasks is shuffled.\n- *Fragility*: The degree to which performance deteriorates or becomes unstable due to seemingly minor perturbations. For example, if a single early task failure leads to repeated propagation of the same mistaken behavior due to inaccurate memory storage, this is fragility.\n- *Implicit curriculum*: Unintentional patterns where easy tasks precede harder ones, as opposed to shuffled orderings.\n- *Memory drift*: The gradual buildup of incorrect, ambiguous, or irrelevant memory entries, which can degrade future behaviors. For example, repeated small misinterpretations accumulate, causing the agent to deviate more over time and to make correlated mistakes on later, similar tasks.\n- *Failure modes*: Systematic breakdowns in performance, often traceable to issues such as ambiguous memory content, misattributed feedback, or poor retrieval dynamics. For instance, an agent might repeatedly fail a class of subtasks because its retrieved memory is based on misleading prior episodes.\n- *Granular feedback*: Structuring feedback at the subtask or finer event granularity, rather than only providing high-level binary success/failure. An example would be flagging partial action sequences for specific correction (\"picked up correct item, then wrong destination\"), enabling more targeted memory correction.\n\n---\n**Research Questions**\n\nWe focus on three major research questions:\n1. To what extent do memory-based agents exhibit consistent performance across repeated runs or varied task orderings?\n2. What are the underlying drivers and patterns of fragility or instability (failure modes) in agents\u2019 improvements over time?\n3. Can interventions in memory or feedback structuring (e.g., rubrics or more granular feedback) effectively reduce such weaknesses, and what limitations remain?\n\n---\n**Experimental Design Overview**\n\n*Agent Architectures and Rationale*\n\nWe examine two established memory-based agent types, selected to represent distinct strategies for encoding and accessing past experiences:\n\n| Agent Type                        | Memory Content & Storage                                     | Memory Usage                                       | Notable Limitations                   |\n|------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------|\n| *Textual episodic memory agent*    | Stores natural-language summaries of actions, errors, corrections after each episode, appended sequentially to persistent memory. | Summaries are retrieved as a reference when facing new tasks; e.g., after an error, the agent consults past mistake summaries.      | Potentially limited by ambiguity or relevance drift in free-text summaries. |\n| *RL agent with memory retrieval*   | Logs structured episode traces: state descriptions, actions, rewards, and feedback, all in text form.               | Prior to acting, retrieves top-k most similar past episodes (measured via embedding-based similarity with current task context), which inform action selection via a Transformer. | Higher computational cost; sensitive to retrieval quality and memory noise. |\n\nThe *textual episodic memory agent* (Wang et al., 2023) produces concise, human-readable summaries after each episode (e.g., \"Moved red block to blue square, but forgot to ring bell\u2014try ringing immediately after\"). This approach simplifies memory storage but may propagate ambiguous or imprecise records, leading to drifting guidance. In contrast, the *RL agent with memory retrieval* (Lee et al., 2022) logs every step structurally (e.g., state transitions, rewards, explicit feedback), enabling more granular lookup and support for multi-step reasoning. However, this comes at the cost of increased memory size and susceptibility to retrieval errors if older, less relevant episodes dominate.\n\nBoth agents use a 6-layer Transformer (512 hidden units per layer, 8 attention heads, GELU activations). These model choices (stacked depth and multi-head attention) were made following prior work demonstrating improved generalization and stability for instruction-following tasks\u2014features critical for evaluating consistency and robustness. Transformers, in particular, allow flexible conditioning on retrieved memories but can be more fragile if presented with irrelevant or redundant memory content.\n\n*Hyperparameters and Model Stability*\n\nHyperparameters\u2014such as memory retrieval k=5, learning rates, and memory window sizes\u2014were systematically tuned for stability of early learning, avoidance of catastrophic forgetting, and to ensure computational tractability for large-scale runs. We specifically chose a sliding memory window of the most recent 100 episodes, balancing the retention of relevant context against increased retrieval cost and overfitting risk observed with larger windows. Settings are held constant across all experimental conditions, with deviations only for explicit intervention studies. Hyperparameter justification and ablation outcomes relevant to reliability metrics are detailed in the Supplement.\n\n*Experimental Controls and Seeds*\n\nRuns use 10 random seeds per agent/configuration (n=2000 episodes per condition), to enable robust estimation of performance variance due to initialization, memory stochasticity, and task ordering. Task generation seeds are also fixed except for experiments testing robustness to order/randomization. This design aids comparability by preventing spurious outcome differences due to random sampling or environmental change.\n\n---\n**Task Environments**\n\n*Task Structure and Difficulty Control*\n\nAgents are evaluated in two environment types, using a parameterized template system to ensure balance:\n\n- *Procedural tasks*: Agents interact within a 5\u00d75 grid-world, following compositional instructions using a controlled 50-word vocabulary. Task complexity is modulated by both subgoal count and distractor presence. Each task is labeled as easy (single-step), medium (2\u20133 steps), or hard (4+ steps); batches are assembled so that task difficulty and type are evenly distributed across experimental conditions and random seeds.\n\n- *Reasoning tasks*: Agents solve multi-step logic or arithmetic problems built from templates, such as \"If you start with 4 coins, double them, give away 3, and add 2, how many left?\" The number and dependency of operations is adjusted for difficulty level, with stratified sampling ensuring balanced exposure.\n\nFor both environments, task generation enforces that, within each batch of episodes, the ratio of easy/medium/hard tasks is maintained (details in Supplement Table S1). This systematic balancing supports cross-condition comparisons without confounds from differing difficulty composition.\n\n---\n**Evaluation Metrics and Statistical Analysis**\n\n*Metrics*\n\n- *Task completion rate*: Percentage of episodes achieving defined goals (per environment/difficulty).\n- *Average episode reward*: Mean sum of subgoal rewards per episode.\n- *Qualitative error analysis*: Categorization of frequent or impactful mistakes using manual and automated log examination.\n\n*Statistical Approach and Justification*\n\nEach reported outcome is aggregated over 10 seeds \u00d7 200 episodes per configuration. Task order is shuffled unless curriculum baselines are specified; difficulty proportions are controlled as above. For group comparisons (e.g., agent types, curricula, interventions), we use Welch\u2019s t-test (preferred over ordinary t-test due to variable sample variance observed across high-variance runs and occasional unequal episode numbers). All p-values are exact unless <0.01; Holm-Bonferroni correction addresses multiple testing.\n\nAssumptions for normality and variance equality were checked (Kolmogorov-Smirnov and Levene\u2019s tests); no substantive violations were found requiring nonparametric alternatives. Effect sizes (Cohen\u2019s d) and 95% confidence intervals (CI) are provided, with practical significance described alongside statistical values. Detailed statistics, including parameter counts and splits, are provided in Table S2 (Supplement).\n\n*Controlling Confounds*\n\nOnly agent and intervention variables are altered between conditions. Environments, task distributions, and episode structures are strictly fixed otherwise, minimizing unintentional sources of variation. Some minor variance due to randomization is acknowledged.\n\n---\n**Results**\n\n*Consistency, Robustness, and Effects of Task Order*\n\nAgents\u2019 task completion rates vary by both random seed and task order. For procedural tasks, rates across seeds range from 55% to 88% (mean 68%, SD 13.7%, 95% CI [66.1%, 69.9%]): indicating substantial inconsistency, particularly under randomized orderings. For reasoning tasks, rates span"}}